{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553400-wyrm-infernalism-and-other-evil-things-same-or-different-things","id":1553400,"title":"Wyrm, Infernalism and other \" evil \" things: Same or different things?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-08-23T08:56:13-07:00","id":1553400,"title":"Wyrm, Infernalism and other \" evil \" things: Same or different things?","content":"In the World of Darkness, there is a lot of &quot; evil &quot; things.<br /> <br /> In Werewolf the Apocalypse, there is the Wyrm corrupts most things on it's path.<br /> <br /> In Vampire the Masquerade, there is some (rare) infernal/demonic vampires and powers.<br /> <br /> In Mage the Ascension, there is the Nephandis.<br /> <br /> In Changeling the Dreaming, there is the Fomorians and probably others. (I might be wrong on this. Pls correct me if need be.)<br /> <br /> There is even a whole game called Demon the Fallen.<br /> <br /> And I surely misses a few other main actors in the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> <br /> So here are my two main questions:<br /> <br /> <br /> 1- There seems to be a lot of demonic and infernal stuff. Are they all from the same &quot; source &quot;?<br /> <br /> 2- Is the Wyrm different from demonic and infernal stuff? (I would say yes, but I might be wrong. Pls correct me if need be.)<br /> <br /> <br /> As always, thanks in advance for your time!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25337-lightbringer1","name":"Lightbringer1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25337_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-23T09:46:58-07:00","id":1553401,"title":"","content":"The games are written to be flexible on this. While some fit better together than others, you can run the setting as if these are all drawing from the same base source (KotE adds a lot more of this into VtM btw), you can also treat them as metaphysically separate.<br /> <br /> So... there's no distinct &quot;right&quot; answer to your question. If you'd like we can offer up how to reconcile or keep distinct the different game's &quot;evil&quot; factions, but in the end this is a part of the WoD that's up to the table to decide how they want to treat it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-23T09:55:19-07:00","id":1553402,"title":"","content":"The short answer to both questions is &quot;it depends on the Storyteller.&quot;<br /> <br /> Part of the &quot;problem&quot; is that while (depending on the writer/time period) there was some intent for the worlds of different WoD games to overlap, it's also the case that each game was designed to play on its own, with their own distinctive concepts and themes. And that led to each game having its own take on demons or demon-like things, designed to fit the story of that particular game.<br /> <br /> The result is that this particular field gets very messy when you start doing crossover between the different games. Some concepts mesh better than others, but there are almost always issues that need to be resolved for which there is no official answer.<br /> <br /> Superficially, each kind of &quot;demon&quot;/evil thing is its own thing. Whether or not there is some kind of underlying connection, and the details of that connection, is something each Storyteller has to decide for their own version of the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-23T13:45:39-07:00","id":1553405,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I was asking because I am currently reading \u201c The Chaos Factor \u201c. (A horribly epic adventure, I know\u2026)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">(Not only is Samuel Haight a joke (figuratively and literally), but this whole adventure is laughingly outstanding.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">(Man, I laughed so much while reading on the 2 technocrats progenitors; the douchebag one and the one who managed to fuse some yeast with the dna of a subject which made her grow exponentially.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">That being said, the Underbelly of the Wyrm has Black Spiral Dancers, Nephandis and Nosferatu Antitribu all working together. So it looks like the Wyrm and the Infernalist are working together in this adventure.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">So this made me wondering if the Wyrm and Infernalist came from the same \u201c evil source \u201c.</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25337-lightbringer1","name":"Lightbringer1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25337_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-23T15:02:35-07:00","id":1553411,"title":"","content":"&quot;Bad Guy Team-ups&quot; work in the WoD on the ground level, regardless of higher explanation, because the antagonists of various lines are almost universally willing to screw over each other to get ahead or otherwise act in a very &quot;ends justify my getting more power&quot; manner. They're not really teaming up; they either have a common goal that smooths over some of the deeper reasons for fighting immediately, or else are willing to work with anyone to get ahead and there will be a sudden - but inevitable - betrayal of one or more sides against the other in the future.<br /> <br /> That side, The Chaos Factor? That explains all the problems right there. That wasn't a book written to be true to the writing of any given line, it was just there to be a big dumb action movie with some horror themes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-23T17:07:34-07:00","id":1553416,"title":"","content":"While I fully endorse that it largely depends, I want to add some observations on each &quot;source&quot;.<br /> <br /> <b>The Wyrm</b> is a powerful (immensely powerful) spirit from the Middle Umbra related to corruption. You can understand its brand of corruption looking up the concept of &quot;kegare&quot; from Japan. The main theme with it are pollution, infection, unhealthy mutations and suffering as a self-replicating problem (the Wyrm itself is said to be mostly a victim of circumstances that can't help but do evil, instead of being deliberately evil, although that doesn't necessarily applies to its servants). It's also important to notice that, if you're using the <b>Triat</b> as a concept in your game, then everything will bear the mark of one of the three, and then every demonic force is likely to be Wyrm-Tainted even if not really Wyrm-related.<br /> <br /> <b>Fallen</b> from the game Demon are Miltonian demons writ large, with the ambiguity of their rebellion greatly emphasized. In practical terms, that means those demons aren't necessarily evil, though there's a lot of reasons for them to be (mostly, and that's an interesting point in the game, <b>PTSD</b>, which their <b>Torment</b> trait isn't just a representation of, but arguably an actual measure). That said, they seem to be unrelated to other demonic forces and have been mostly imprisoned for most of history, with very few getting out of Hell until 1999. For whatever it's worth, they're all Wyrm-Tainted even if not all Wyrm allies (and quite a number are its enemies, for one reason or another) and some are even good people.<br /> <br /> <b>Spirits</b> of all sorts, and even gods, can also be demons. Mage is the line that goes on more details about such beings. Beyond the Wyrm, there are Astral Realms, Everett Volumes, Horizon and beyond Realms, there are Godheads, Avatars, Bygones, all those things may be evil/demonic in nature, and all of them may be related or completely independent.<br /> <br /> <b>Horrors</b> <b>Cosmic </b>also exist in the setting, more prominently in Mage and Wraith. Things from outside space and time, things from before the universe, darkness given consciousness (or something that passes as such), alien, uncaring monsters too big in scope to relate to. Those may also be demonic or otherwise the source of Evil, by nature or accident.<br /> <br /> Changeling's <b>Fomorians</b> are a special case, as they have never been really used as a source of evil even if that was the original intent. They are the original fey creatures, and that's as far as we know about them even today. No confirmed servants, no confirmed activity, and while they represent <i>fear</i> and <i>nightmare</i>, they're not the only sources of those. Far from it, Changeling has a lingering problem that I like to call its <i>50 shades of Nightmare</i>, where for a time supplements tried constantly to outdo themselves in this regard, and the result is a plethora of Gallain (and Kith) to explore the very same idea. At the end of the day, Changeling really doesn't mesh well with the idea of a source of EVIL, so it's usually outside those discussions.<br /> <br /> Now, <b>followers of Evil</b> vary a lot, but they may worship or draw power from any of those sources. Mage in particular plays on this a lot, so you have several possible &quot;flavors&quot; of Nefandi (without an &quot;s&quot;, this is already plural) that may work with each-other, but are usually distinct in some way. Vampire does the same but opposite, where Infernalists can't tell the difference, even if they think they do. Overall, though, evil is evil, those entities will ally with anything if it advances their personal aspirations, fight anything that gets in their way and betray any and all allies as soon as it's beneficial for them personally, give or take some crazy zealots being crazy loyal. That not only has nothing to do with a common source, they'll be like that even within a given group.<br /> <br /> And finally, <i>The Chaos Factor</i> is a mess with very little value to understand the setting, despite being &quot;canon&quot;, as far as it goes. It's also the last stand of Sam Haight, and yes, he's a joke, but a joke that was haunting the books for some time (this was his eighth appearance if I'm not mistaken). He died in this adventure, became a Wraith (a normal one), and ended up forged into an ashtray. An ashtray that would still make two or three more appearances. His <b>Skin Dancers</b> are still a thing in the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-23T19:35:04-07:00","id":1553418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553400-wyrm-infernalism-and-other-evil-things-same-or-different-things?p=1553416#post1553416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>Spirits</b> of all sorts, and even gods, can also be demons. Mage is the line that goes on more details about such beings.</u> Beyond the Wyrm, there are Astral Realms, Everett Volumes, Horizon and beyond Realms, there are Godheads, Avatars, Bygones, all those things may be evil/demonic in nature, and all of them may be related or completely independent.</div> </div> </div> </div><sup>(underline mine)</sup><br /> <br /> Is there a particular source you can recommend for more information on this concept?<br /> <br /> Excellent summation!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-23T21:47:07-07:00","id":1553419,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kharnov</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553400-wyrm-infernalism-and-other-evil-things-same-or-different-things?p=1553418#post1553418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Is there a particular source you can recommend for more information on this concept?<br /> <br /> Excellent summation!</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks!<br /> <br /> Your best shot is probably <i>The Infinite Tapestry</i> for the main Revised work on the matter, plus <i>The Book of Worlds</i> if you want to take a look at earlier material. But I think you really have a very serviceable introduction in M20 <i>Gods &amp; Monsters</i>, as it will deal less with the Umbra itself and more with all those types of entities, including the ones who do not live in the Umbra, and it will be as didactic about them as is possible.<br /> <br /> Long story short, there are all sorts of entities in Mage that don't occur in other lines. Those they more commonly call demons come from the Astral Reaches, a part of the Umbra no other line explores, but things that may or may not be <b>THE</b>, or at leas <b>A</b> source of <b>Evil</b> may come from several other places in the Umbra that other lines don't explore, either. And finally, you have the last three classifications I cited:<br /> <br /> Godheads interact in their own terms, don't seem to have any limit to their power and are not tied to any particular realm of existence as far as anyone knows, they may be just powerful spirits, but they may also exist simultaneously on infinite versions of reality, in such a way that the very end of the world means nothing to them, or even just be a case of mass hysteria. Avatars are only truly known in their manifestation as a companion to a Mage, but we actually have no hint at what they really are and the limits of their ability to independently interact with the world. Bygones are all sorts of beings that are natives to the Material world, but can't exist in &quot;regular&quot; Reality Zones because the Consensus harms them.<br /> <br /> None of those entities has a definitive explanation on what they are or how they relate to the world at large, so you can explore them in many ways, including as demons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-24T03:20:22-07:00","id":1553423,"title":"","content":"I want to thank everyone for your answer. Its really nice!<br /> <br /> Also, I want to specify that I understand that in a settings like a World of Darkness, &quot; evil &quot; can mean a lot of things.<br /> <br /> And to think of it, I shouldn't have use this word in the beginning.<br /> <br /> Also its not a binary thing.<br /> <br /> We are not in Dungeons and Dragons were in some settings, Good and Evil are actual forces / entities / gods that exist and clashes against each other through their clerics and other representatives. In WoD, there are a lot a grey zones, and it's more subjective than objective, even though we can all agree that a Baali is not a nice character, for example.<br /> <br /> Two people having an argument are not manipulated by the Wyrm or by some infernal forces.<br /> <br /> I was mainly curious to read your opinions wether or not if the Wyrm and infernal stuff were the same thing.<br /> <br /> I think I like the idea that they are not, although a Nephandi doing his usual stuff might leave a &quot; wyrmish &quot; resonance, for example, it doesn't mean that the wyrm is behind him.<br /> <br /> And in the other way, a Black Spiral Dancer doing his stuff is not doesn't mean a Baali or other infernal actor is behind his actions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25337-lightbringer1","name":"Lightbringer1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25337_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-24T10:11:23-07:00","id":1553429,"title":"","content":"I think we all understood what you meant. And that's why I used Evil instead of evil, it was on purpose.<br /> <br /> While the settings are mostly about grey morality, there are objectively bad stuff, in the sense that they can't be anything but evil and/or carry some sort of supernatural corruption that turns things and people to evil. Even a Methuselah vampire is just evil, but an infernalist is usually Evil. The setting also has the notion that while &quot;normal people&quot; can be plenty evil, no one tolerates Evil. That's why <i>The Chaos Factor</i> puts all those things together, they're all Evil regardless if they're related or not, so everyone else is whiling to put their differences aside to fight them.<br /> <br /> That isn't at all common in the setting, but does actually exist.<br /> <br /> Fallen and Changelings are special cases because of some particularities. Demon still carries the idea that those entities are the things behind infernal pacts and whatnot, so while they're brought to the morally grey area, they're still strongly linked to this kind of corruption. There <b>are</b> demons who are every bit as Evil as you can think, and that spread strife, chaos and corruption where they go, just for the giggles. There aren't just demons that fit the bill of diabolical entities, the game makes it clear that the entities infernalists and Nefandi worship may very well be Fallen. Above all, the game gives players all the tools to <b>be</b> such entities, and to make it clear even the best Fallen are just a bad day from becoming like that (and they also have the tools for it).<br /> <br /> Changelings have an entirely different relationship with Evil because it isn't actually a bad thing for them. Being a nightmare is acceptable in their society, being a villain is perfectly fine. As long as you don't kill other fae with Cold Iron, anything goes. They're living stories, and stories need all roles, so the only thing that keeps evil Changelings from taking over the world for evil's sake are good Changelings trying to thwart them for good's sake, all the while both are equally accepted in their society <i>as they are</i>. In fact, as much as other splats are monsters, Changeling is the only one where a starting character can openly eat children alive for lunch and dine watching live impalement every night without any drawback whatsoever, mechanical or social.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-24T17:18:17-07:00","id":1553435,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> Thanks! I have read The Book of Worlds and Infinite Tapestry, but it was a while ago, and I was more focused on the structure of the Otherworlds so it may have not really stuck in my mind when I read it. Still haven't read Gods and Monsters, so that'll have to be on the list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-25T11:36:02-07:00","id":1553444,"title":"","content":"This is how I run it in my chronicles.<br /> <br /> There is a division between the &quot;powers of darkness&quot; in the sense that certain monsters, cults, etc. believe in different mythologies and organize things according to their beliefs. Sometimes specific monsters, spirits, etc. easily fall into one category or another, or are difficult to do so. Other than that is what those people believe to be true, such categories are meaningless from any objective standard unless we are talking about connections to one of the levels of &quot;reality&quot;. The Fomorians exist in the Dreaming so it makes sense to reference that. Malfeas as an Umbral Domain exists so it makes sense to refer to its denizens as Malfeans. There are (many) realms of Hell and such in the Astral Plane/High Umbra. The Labyrinth does exist. However, what may connect them at a higher spiritual level is not known. A &quot;demon&quot; haunting someone in the material world could be any number of things.<br /> <br /> The &quot;Wyrm&quot; as a distinct entity is part of Werewolf cosmology. It's what the Changing Breeds believe and because of their ubiquity and antiquity, lots of other people use the same terms. However, Much of Garou mythology is just that, mythology. They really don't know anything more about the ultimate evil than anybody else (though they may know more about various evil spirits and beings at a much lower level).<br /> <br /> The Triat of the Garou is anthropomorphized version of Mage's Metaphysical Trinity. Their stories about the Balance Wyrm and its transformation into the Wyrm is just how they deal with the philosophical Problem of Evil.<br /> <br /> The Neverborn of Wraith are more metaphysically &quot;real&quot; than the Nephandic entities and other evil spiritual entities. They are the dreams of the Neverborn (as per the Book of Mirrors in Mage). They are a much &quot;higher&quot; form of evil/destruction since they predate the act of Creation.<br /> <br /> The ultimate true origin of Evil is left vague just as is the nature of the One/Prime, or whatever you want to call it. Most religions, fictional game splats, etc. contain varying amounts of insight into it. Whether the prime evil is Satan, the Wyrm, Ahriman, the false nature of Reality/Maya, a flaw in creation, Morgoth, Oblivion, or something else is best left to the ST, but is probably immaterial to most chronicles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-25T14:04:38-07:00","id":1553449,"title":"","content":"While a small nitpick in terms of the broader topic, it feels a bit dismissive to the Garou to both make their mythology subordinate to Mage's cosmology, and to assert that they even need to address the Problem of Evil; when the Problem of Evil is only really valid as an argument against a strong monotheistic deity that is tri-omni.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-25T21:44:09-07:00","id":1553454,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553400-wyrm-infernalism-and-other-evil-things-same-or-different-things?p=1553444#post1553444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The &quot;Wyrm&quot; as a distinct entity is part of Werewolf cosmology. It's what the Changing Breeds believe and because of their ubiquity and antiquity, lots of other people use the same terms. However, Much of Garou mythology is just that, mythology. They really don't know anything more about the ultimate evil than anybody else (though they may know more about various evil spirits and beings at a much lower level).</div> </div> </div> </div>Is that why there's a capstone end book for the original line dedicated to resolving all of the points of their world-concepts as if they were, in fact, 100% true?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Triat of the Garou is anthropomorphized version of Mage's Metaphysical Trinity. Their stories about the Balance Wyrm and its transformation into the Wyrm is just how they deal with the philosophical Problem of Evil. </div> </div> </div>The Triat isn't really very anthropomorphized outside of in-character tales and stores that get passed around by Garou and Fera. Developer notes from later into the original run of W:tA didn't liken it to Vader or Palpatine, but the entire Dark Side of the Force.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-27T10:20:15-07:00","id":1553485,"title":"","content":"One of the major themes I've noted about World of Darkness is that the Bad Guys\u2122 are always working together and get along, while the Good Guys\u2122 struggle to do so. It's very much a setting where everything gets worse because of a lack of compromise. So, in that vein, when I run a game I have the bad guys all working together even if they might have slightly different end goals and slightly different reasons for things. I like to take a note from Dresden Files when it comes to evil: the bad guys work together because they want to destroy it, what they do after is where they have their disagreements. <br /> That said, I have a lot of the bad guys be tangentially related. The Wyrm and Hell are both things, both with similar goals, but they are distinctive things with their own rules. <i>Book of Madness</i> had the Nephandi be sorted into multiple types: Wyrm, Hell, and Lords of the Outer Night. That last one is never espoused on in the lore, but I believe it's related to the Deep Umbra and Things Best Not Spoken Of\u2122. <br /> If you're wondering how much of it should be true as the GM, my suggestion is always what the old books recommended: whatever is the splat you're playing is the correct lore. Werewolf? Wyrm and Wyrm Accessories. Mage? Everything is true. Demon? Earthbound. Vampire? It's all just Evil Demon Shit\u2122 and the players shouldn't interact with the details unless they want to get into a bad situation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-05T04:28:51-07:00","id":1553638,"title":"","content":"In the 1st Edition days, back when World of Darkness was still called &quot;The Storyteller Series&quot; and Wraith and Changeling were going to be called &quot;Ghost&quot; and &quot;Faerie&quot;, it was heavily implied (if not outright stated in a few places) that The Wyrm and the Infernal/Satanic were damn near synonymous. It was very much implied that the Wyrm's servants, the Nephandi, the Baali, and the various demons seen up to that point were all in service of the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> Granted, 1e was a lot more open to the concept of crossovers while later editions went in a more ambiguous direction and now in the Legacy/Classic World of Darkness (I'm not counting the absolute dreck that is 5th Edition), it's basically up to what the ST decides.<br /> <br /> On a tangential side note, I find it fascinating that the games became more hostile to crossovers while their metaplot conversely became more overbearing, it's kind of ironic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm just a trans guy and gaming fan, unabashed hedonist and unusually upbeat nihilist, fairly laid back. He/Him.<br /> <br /> If everything is political, then nothing is!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25920-matt-corleone","name":"Matt Corleone","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25920_8.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-05T05:26:28-07:00","id":1553639,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt Corleone</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553400-wyrm-infernalism-and-other-evil-things-same-or-different-things?p=1553638#post1553638\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In the 1st Edition days, back when World of Darkness was still called &quot;The Storyteller Series&quot; and Wraith and Changeling were going to be called &quot;Ghost&quot; and &quot;Faerie&quot;, it was heavily implied (if not outright stated in a few places) that The Wyrm and the Infernal/Satanic were damn near synonymous. It was very much implied that the Wyrm's servants, the Nephandi, the Baali, and the various demons seen up to that point were all in service of the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> Granted, 1e was a lot more open to the concept of crossovers while later editions went in a more ambiguous direction and now in the Legacy/Classic World of Darkness (I'm not counting the absolute dreck that is 5th Edition), it's basically up to what the ST decides.<br /> <br /> On a tangential side note, I find it fascinating that the games became more hostile to crossovers while their metaplot conversely became more overbearing, it's kind of ironic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Vampire was always insufferably overbearing and had horrid &quot;I have more dots in this, I win! La la la I'm not listening!&quot; crossover &quot;rules&quot;. The hostility toward crossover was just making sure that attitude didn't run roughshod over every other game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-05T06:55:23-07:00","id":1553641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553400-wyrm-infernalism-and-other-evil-things-same-or-different-things?p=1553639#post1553639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Vampire was always insufferably overbearing and had horrid &quot;I have more dots in this, I win! La la la I'm not listening!&quot; crossover &quot;rules&quot;. The hostility toward crossover was just making sure that attitude didn't run roughshod over every other game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, I think some of it was that and some of the crossover hostility in Revised was also a backlash to Samuel Haight's wacky misadventures or towards the (IMHO) honestly underrated and overhated Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand. Basically, the same &quot;Personal Horror Is The One True Way! No Badwrongfun Allowed!&quot; ethos that would eventually dominate the WoD IP entirely once Paradox took control.<br /> <br /> Like I mentioned before, it is kind of ironic that the Revised era became a lot more crossover-hostile (there's a thread on RPG.net where one of the old devs mentions that each of the later Revised gamelines were being developed with the mindset of being self-contained) while also heavily relying on a metaplot that was dependent on crossovers (particularly crossovers with Wraith), since the last three gamelines of Classic WoD's original run (Hunter, Mummy, and Demon) all relied on The Sixth Great Maelstrom to function.<br /> <br /> The specific reasons for crossover hostility in the later years of Classic WoD were very much a case of &quot;Depending On The Writer&quot; but the end results were still the same.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm just a trans guy and gaming fan, unabashed hedonist and unusually upbeat nihilist, fairly laid back. He/Him.<br /> <br /> If everything is political, then nothing is!","edited":{"userid":25920,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25920-matt-corleone","user":"Matt Corleone","date":". 09-05-2025, 10:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25920-matt-corleone","name":"Matt Corleone","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25920_8.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-05T09:33:35-07:00","id":1553642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553400-wyrm-infernalism-and-other-evil-things-same-or-different-things?p=1553639#post1553639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Vampire was always insufferably overbearing and had horrid &quot;I have more dots in this, I win! La la la I'm not listening!&quot; crossover &quot;rules&quot;. The hostility toward crossover was just making sure that attitude didn't run roughshod over every other game.</div> </div> </div> </div>I personally think this has less to do with Vampire being overbearing and more actually with the very hostility towards crossover. It's precisely because they were hostile to it that Vampire never got corrected (at least as far as Crossover goes).<br /> <br /> But I actually think all those questions: the growing hostility towards crossover, the growing interconnectedness of the Metaplot and the Forces of Evil becoming more distinct, they all derive from the games simply growing over time.<br /> <br /> First, it's natural for any RPG that growth will also mean expanding interpretations and material that increase options, even if they somewhat detract from original stances (if at least it doesn't make them impossible). More possible explanations, more possible factions, more possible outcomes. That's why Golconda went from a completely positive thing to an ever grayer one, as players and STs wanted to explore other possibilities of what it meant. That's why DSotBH was at the same time despised by many, but something out of it was used by nearly everyone. And that's one of the main reasons why the Forces of Evil became more diverse and explored alternative origins.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, their already conflicting cosmologies and distinct rules meant that expanding any game line wasn't conducive of keeping them together, as you have by necessity to expand a cosmology that's already incompatible (to a degree) and unifying rules would lose their native themes. So even outside the <i>Revised </i>movement to curb badwrongfun, the systems were naturally becoming more and more hostile to crossover.<br /> <br /> And last, expanding also means building connections. Even though their cosmologies were growing ever more apart, more and more actual explored connections were built between the games, more and more lore crossovers, because that's always an easy and rewarding path to expand lore.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}